[38;5;231m⏺[39m [1mSecond fork:[0m how [38;5;153mapp.js[39m gets at the settings, given there's no bundler and [38;5;153mindex.html[39m uses a plain [38;5;153m<script>[39m tag.

  - [1mGlobal via a second script tag[0m — [38;5;153msettings.js[39m loads before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m and sets [38;5;153mwindow.APP_SETTINGS[39m. Works exactly like
    the current setup, still opens straight from the filesystem by double-clicking [38;5;153mindex.html[39m. Costs: a global, and load
    order in [38;5;153mindex.html[39m becomes load-bearing. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mES modules[0m — [38;5;153msettings.js[39m does [38;5;153mexport const[39m, [38;5;153mapp.js[39m does [38;5;153mimport[39m, and [38;5;153mindex.html[39m switches to [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m.
    Real module boundary, no globals, and it's where the project would want to end up. Costs: [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m files are
    fetched under CORS rules, so [38;5;153mfile://[39m stops working — you'd need a local server ([38;5;153mnpx serve[39m or similar) to open the
    page at all. That's the part that isn't cheap to undo if someone's workflow is "double-click the HTML".

  [1mMy recommendation: ES modules[0m, unless opening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m directly matters to you. It gives a genuine module with no
  global, and the settings module is the natural moment to make that switch while there are only two files to convert.
  If the file-open workflow matters, the global is the honest choice and costs almost nothing here.
